---
import { getEntry, render } from "astro:content";
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { readFileSync } from "node:fs";
import { resolve } from "node:path";

// The agreement is published here once it is final. While LICENSE.md is still a draft for
// legal review, the page gives the summary only.
const entry = await getEntry("legal", "license");
// The site builds from website/, so the agreement is one level up.
const text = readFileSync(resolve(process.cwd(), "../LICENSE.md"), "utf-8");
const draft = !entry || text.includes("DRAFT");
const agreement = entry && !draft ? await render(entry) : undefined;
---

<Base
  title="Licence"
  description="Drift's licence in plain words: what the end-user licence agreement lets you do."
>
  <div class="wrap page prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">Licence</p>
    <h1>Drift's licence <em>in plain words</em></h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Drift is proprietary software. Its end-user licence agreement lets you install it and use
      it for your work. The drawings you make are yours.
    </p>

    <h2>What you can do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Install Drift on computers you own or control, and use it for anything: your own projects, your firm's drawings, client work, teaching.</li>
      <li>Keep everything you draw with it. Drift claims no rights in your drawings and doesn't send them anywhere.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What you can't do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Copy, share, sell or rent Drift itself, or make it available to others over a network.</li>
      <li>Change Drift, or decompile or reverse engineer it, except where the law allows that.</li>
      <li>Work around a licence key or the limits of the demo.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Check your drawings</h2>
    <p>
      Drift is a drawing tool. Check the dimensions, areas and other results before you rely
      on them for building, making or signing anything.
    </p>

    <h2>Earlier versions</h2>
    <p>
      Versions up to and including Beta 1.5 (0.1.0-beta.1.5) were released under the
      Functional Source License, Version 1.1, ALv2 Future License (FSL-1.1-ALv2). Copies of
      those versions stay under it.
    </p>

    <h2>Other people's code</h2>
    <p>
      Drift is built with open-source libraries and fonts, each under its own licence. They are
      listed, with their licences and copyright notices, in the{" "}
      <a href="/licence/notices/">third-party notices</a>, which also come with every copy of
      Drift and are shown under <strong>About Drift → Licences…</strong>
    </p>

    {
      agreement ? (
        <>
          <h2 id="agreement">The agreement</h2>
          <agreement.Content />
        </>
      ) : (
        <p>
          The full agreement comes with Drift: it is shown when you install it, and is in
          <code>LICENSE.md</code> next to the program.
        </p>
      )
    }

    <blockquote>
      <p>
        This page is a summary to help you understand the licence. It isn't legal advice, and
        if it ever differs from the licence agreement, the agreement is what counts.
      </p>
    </blockquote>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .page {
    padding-top: 56px;
  }
</style>
